Yes, a single website can absolutely use all four API types at the same time.
In modern software development, large websites are rarely built as one single program. Instead, they are built using a microservices architecture, where different parts of the website are treated as independent mini-applications. Each mini-application uses whichever API style is best suited for its specific job. [1, 2] 
Here is a realistic example of how a massive platform (like an e-commerce site or a trading market) would use all four API types simultaneously:
## 1. REST (For Public Integration)

* Where it is used: The public-facing developer portal.
* Why: The website wants external companies, partners, and indie developers to build bots or tools. Since REST is the universal language of the internet, they expose standard endpoints like POST /api/v1/orders so that any basic programming script can easily interact with the platform. [3] 

## 2. GraphQL (For the Main Dashboard Frontend)

* Where it is used: The user's account dashboard or product listing page.
* Why: A user's profile dashboard is complex. It needs to load user details, recent notifications, active listings, and shopping cart items all at once. Instead of making 5 separate REST calls (which slows down the browser), the frontend mobile app or web page sends one clean GraphQL query to fetch all that nested data in a single network round-trip.

## 3. SOAP (For Financial/Payment Processing)

* Where it is used: The legacy payment gateway connection or identity verification (KYC) system.
* Why: The website needs to talk to traditional banking mainframes or credit card networks to process payouts. Because these financial networks were built decades ago and require strict cryptographic contracts and heavy XML logging, the website communicates with them using SOAP. [4] 

## 4. gRPC / RPC (For Fast Internal Communication)

* Where it is used: Deep inside the company's internal server network (behind the firewall).
* Why: When a user buys an item, the Front-End Server needs to immediately tell the Inventory Server to lock the item, tell the Wallet Server to deduct funds, and tell the Notification Server to send an email. Because these servers handle millions of requests per second inside the same data center, they use gRPC because its binary format is incredibly fast and saves massive amounts of server hardware memory.

